## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives (`string`, `number`, `boolean`), change means value change. For objects and functions, change means **reference** change — and a new reference is created on every render.

```jsx
// Bad: new object reference on every parent render
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Bad: new function reference on every parent render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every time the parent renders, `Child` receives a new prop reference even though the value is identical — React sees it as changed and re-renders `Child`.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.

Use it when: a component is expensive to render and its parent re-renders frequently for unrelated reasons.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ value, onClick }) => {
  return <button onClick={onClick}>{value}</button>;
});
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.

Use it when: passing a callback to a `memo`-wrapped child (otherwise `memo` is defeated by a new function ref each render).

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [id]); // only changes when `id` changes
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value across renders.

Use it when: (a) passing an object/array to a `memo`-wrapped child, or (b) the computation itself is genuinely expensive.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red', size: 12 }), []);
```

> **Don't over-memoize.** These have their own cost. Only apply them when you've confirmed a real performance problem or when the prop is going to a `memo`-wrapped child.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context consumer re-renders whenever the context **value** changes reference. Providers that recreate the value object inline are the most common culprit.

```jsx
// Bad: new object on every App render → all consumers re-render
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      <Tree />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fix:** memoize the value, or split contexts so stable values don't change with volatile ones.

```jsx
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={value}>
      <Tree />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Or split into `UserContext` (changes) and `UserDispatchContext` (stable `setUser`), so components that only dispatch never re-render on user changes.

---

### 4. Before/After Example

**Before — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:**

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleSelect = (item) => console.log('selected:', item);

  // New array reference every render
  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.includes(query));

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  console.log('ExpensiveList rendered'); // fires on every keystroke
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
}
```

**After — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `filtered` actually changes:**

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']);

  // Stable function reference
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => {
    console.log('selected:', item);
  }, []);

  // Only recomputes when query or items changes
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.includes(query)),
    [items, query]
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Skips render when props are shallowly equal
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  console.log('ExpensiveList rendered'); // only fires when filtered changes
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
});
```

---

### Quick Decision Guide

| Situation | Tool |
|---|---|
| Child renders too often from parent re-renders | `React.memo` |
| Passing a callback to a memo'd child | `useCallback` |
| Passing an object/array to a memo'd child | `useMemo` |
| Context value recreated on every render | `useMemo` on the value |
| Consumers of different context slices | Split contexts |
